1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,072
Fees can reduce what a company

2
00:00:02,072 --> 00:00:05,250
receives or what an investor
ultimately keeps.

3
00:00:05,250 --> 00:00:08,955
Depending on the offering, there
may be a platform fee,

4
00:00:08,955 --> 00:00:12,996
payment-processing charge,
administrative fee, fund expense,

5
00:00:12,996 --> 00:00:14,612
or other disclosed cost.

6
00:00:14,612 --> 00:00:19,300
The issuer may pay some fees,
while an investor may pay others.

7
00:00:19,300 --> 00:00:23,125
Do not assume one fee model
applies to every offering.

8
00:00:23,125 --> 00:00:27,073
Read the fee table, subscription
documents, and payment screen,

9
00:00:27,073 --> 00:00:31,085
and distinguish a fee charged on
top of your investment from one

10
00:00:31,085 --> 00:00:32,150
deducted from it.

11
00:00:32,150 --> 00:00:37,851
In a fictional example, a $1,000
commitment with a 3% investor

12
00:00:37,851 --> 00:00:40,242
fee plus a $1 fixed charge

13
00:00:40,242 --> 00:00:44,012
would add $31, making the
payment $1,031.

14
00:00:44,012 --> 00:00:48,938
If instead the same charges were
deducted from the $1,000, the

15
00:00:48,938 --> 00:00:52,512
amount reaching the investment
would be $969.

16
00:00:52,512 --> 00:00:56,200
The exact calculation depends on
the documents.

17
00:00:56,200 --> 00:01:00,150
Ask whether any ongoing costs
apply after the offering closes

18
00:01:00,150 --> 00:01:04,488
and whether a fee is refundable if
the raise fails or is cancelled.

